  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 254 AM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nAs the seasonally nebulous pattern continues into early next\nweek, broad western-U.S. ridging should keep the heat around for\nat least Monday and likely into Tuesday (particularly for the\nsouthern half of the CWA). However, with time, perturbations\nembedded in the northern-U.S. northwest flow will chip away at\nthe eastern extent of the ridge, allowing for closer intrusions\nof attendant convection and cooler air upstream of the\nperturbations. Of course, model guidance is exhibiting a lot of\nspread in the details by this point, particularly with timing of\nshortwave troughs in the northwest flow. Thus, confidence\nremains rather low with the precipitation forecast next week.\n\nHowever, models are hinting at a more active period for the\nregion around the middle to latter portions of next week. With\nperturbation after perturbation digging southeastward into the\nMidwest, multiple phenomena will exist to initiate and maintain\nstorms at times. This process will also shunt the larger-scale\nridging to the west of the region. Cold fronts should\nsuccessfully move into/through the forecast area by later in the\nweek, bringing temperatures back into the cooler 80s as the week\nrolls on. With this aspect of the forecast, confidence is a\nlittle higher, probably around average for a 7-day forecast in\nthe warm season.\n\nOne thing to watch with this evolving pattern is if repeated\nrounds of convection are generated in a geographically confined\nregion. If so, this would increase the flash flooding threat\nwith time. Such a threat may encompass portions of our area.\nHowever, we will need far more agreement and consistency among\nmodel guidance before providing specifics on this potentially\nemerging threat.",
